From 169078a4cc1a04d85a2cab57dc2bb922632fa5ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:52:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 503/503] [DS4.1] Indexer scores read no key past the visible
 positions

DeepSeek V4.1's reference indexer reads index_k[:, : end_pos // ratio]
and nothing past it. The TileLang score kernel loaded whole 64-row tiles,
so the last tile's rows past a row's extent read keys and scale words of
slots nobody had written (the rest of the last visible position's page),
then discarded their scores at the store. Those rows' key and scale loads
are now predicated off and they score zeros, which the store still drops.
Only the loads are predicated: the page read stays unconditional, so a dense
tile's addresses stay affine and its 32 loads a thread still issue together.
The extent joins the bounds check TileLang already puts on each load; nvcc
had unswitched the release's uniform form of that check into branches, and
the per-row form compiles straight-line (127 registers; the release's 124).
Candidate mode is unchanged: its source layer writes the list whole, -1
padded, so rows past a list's length load position 0, which is visible, as
a masked candidate does. Stored scores are bit for bit as before: rows are
independent in the block-scaled MMA, and the rows that changed are the ones
the store drops.

Tests: compute-sanitizer's initcheck scores a cache written only at the
visible positions (dense and by candidates, 128- and 256-row pages, extents
inside a tile) and must report no read of the rest; skipped where no
sanitizer is found (COMPUTE_SANITIZER names one). NaN bytes in every slot
past the extent leave every stored score bit-identical.
benchmark_deepseek_v41_indexer_bound.py times the kernel against the
release's at 1M-context depths, dense and by candidates, after a
bit-for-bit comparison, and gates on it being slower at no case.
